{"episodes":{"184662":"A couple of microbursts occurred in association with intense thunderstorm cells that formed in the heat of the afternoon. The storms developed during the heat of the afternoon ahead of a strong cold front that trailed south-southwest from southern Minnesota to extreme eastern Kansas. An increasing southwest low-level flow of moist and unstable air provided fuel for the storms. The storms evolved into a small but slow-moving complex over the Pennyrile region of west Kentucky. This complex produced very heavy rainfall that resulted in isolated flash flooding.","184755":"A line of showers and thunderstorms moved into northern Utah during the evening hours, resulting in isolated instances of strong wind gusts before dissipating.","184840":"An upper disturbance moving through a moist and unstable airmass spawned a line of severe thunderstorms that caused damage over Isle Royale National Park on the evening of the 5th.","184849":"A weak low pressure system and cold front helped to generate strong storms in north central and south central South Dakota during the late afternoon and evening hours of September 4th. The strongest recorded wind gust with any of these storms was 70 mph on a personal weather station at a residence 7 miles south of Morristown, SD.","184884":"Scattered strong to severe thunderstorms developed across portions of the region with a localized microburst observed near Westbrook, Texas.","184996":"A bowing line of thunderstorms traversed across ND into NW MN during the late night hours of September 4th - early morning September 5th. High winds and wind damage were the main impacts.","184997":"A bowing line of thunderstorms traversed across ND into NW MN during the late night hours of September 4th - early morning September 5th. High winds and wind damage were the main impacts.","185529":"A slowly advancing cold front, roughly paralleling the Interstate 44 corridor, helped initiate widely scattered convection during early evening hours on the 5th. While very hot surface temperatures aided in the development of significant convective (and downdraft) potential instability, weak deep-layer wind shear fostered primarily disorganized (single-/multi-cellular) convection. Downburst wind events, including a cluster of such events just east of the Oklahoma City Metropolitan area, were noted with the most robust convection during the evening.","185621":"An area of thunderstorms stretching from far southeast Montana towards southwest South Dakota developed ahead of an upper level trough, which moved to the northeast towards North Dakota. As the storms pushed into North Dakota, they encountered an atmosphere of elevated instability and strong deep layer shear. Strong and damaging wind gusts quickly became the key threat with these storms as they progressed to the northeast, though a few storms did produce large hail, with the largest hail being half-dollar size in McLean County. Multiple events of wind gusts over 70 mph occurred. The strongest wind gust estimated at 100 mph occurred in far northwest Kidder County, where neighboring farmsteads sustained heavy damage with a destroyed mobile home and grain bin, a severely damaged shop building, and multiple large trees either uprooted or snapped.","185639":"Scattered thunderstorms developed, along a stationary front situated in the Nebraska Panhandle, during the early evening hours of September 4th. As this activity pushed east into western Nebraska, it intensified producing strong wind gusts at North Platte and Valentine. Wind gusts up to 76 MPH were reported in North Platte with 62 MPH wind gusts reported in Valentine.","185694":"Scattered thunderstorms occurred across southeast Montana, with a couple producing quarter size hail and a severe wind gust.","185842":"Thunderstorms developed ahead of a cold front moving across northern Minnesota during the afternoon hours of the 5th. Storms along the Iron Range of northeastern Minnesota were quite strong and produced damaging winds from the Hibbing area northeast to the Isabella area. Widespread tree damage was observed along with concentrated corridors of more significant damage. NWS meteorologists performed a storm survey of the hardest hit areas near Aurora and Hoyt Lakes and found damage indicative of straight line winds in excess of 90 mph. Additional storms were seen elsewhere across the region that produced minor tree damage and marginally severe hail.","185855":"An upper low centered over West Tennessee resulted in scattered showers and thunderstorms across the region.  Suface-based convective available potential energy values of around 3000 J/kg and 0-6 km bulk shear values of 30 knots supported a couple of severe storms with large hail and damaging winds.","185856":"Thunderstorms developed ahead of a cold front moving across northern Minnesota during the afternoon hours of the 5th. Areas of wind damage were reported across the Iron Range and Minnesota Arrowhead as the moved to the east. As these storms moved over western Lake Superior, they continued to produce strong winds with a measured gust to 70 mph being recorded by an ore boat traversing the open waters of Lake Superior south of Lutsen.","186089":"On the evening of September 5th, storms brought large hail to portions of western MO.","186212":"A large area of persistent moderate rain caused fast rising streams and creeks, leading to minor flooding of rural roads across the southern Bighorn Mountains."},"events":[{"begin":"2023-09-05T00:00Z","county":"Morton","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2023-09-05T00:04Z","endLat":46.92,"endLon":-101.86,"episode":185621,"id":1139310,"injuries":0,"lat":46.92,"location":"7.05 NNW Glen Ullin","lon":-101.86,"magnitude":1.0,"source":"Public","state":"ND","type":"Hail","wfo":"BIS"},{"begin":"2023-09-05T00:00Z","county":"Morton","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2023-09-05T00:04Z","endLat":46.92,"endLon":-101.86,"